# Copy to `.env` before running anything else in this checkout (see the
# README's Setup section). `.env` is gitignored; this file is the schema.

# Which backend the tooling in this checkout talks to: 'local' (the
# docker-compose dev server) or 'aws' (PRODUCTION).
#
# Why this exists: without a .env the CLI defaults to production — right for
# an installed tool, wrong inside a checkout. Ships as 'local' so that
# copying this file is what makes the checkout safe; pointing at production
# becomes the deliberate act. Any value other than local/aws refuses to
# start rather than guessing.
#
# Who reads it: the CLI (packages/cli/src/config.ts — loads this file itself,
# no `source` needed; a real exported variable still wins) and DEBUG builds
# of the app (baked in at bundle time by react-native-dotenv, so restart
# Metro to pick up a change). RELEASE builds of the app are hard-wired to aws
# and ignore this file entirely.
TACENDUM_ENV=local

# Individual endpoint overrides; they beat TACENDUM_ENV. Leave EMPTY (empty
# means unset, for the CLI and the app alike) — but do not delete the lines:
# react-native-dotenv only inlines `process.env.X` for keys PRESENT in a .env
# file, so these keys existing here is what lets a shell `export
# TACENDUM_API=…` reach a debug app bundle at all (a non-empty shell export
# beats the file's value; an empty one does not). The CLI honours the same
# two variables with the same precedence without needing this file. Release
# builds of the app ignore them entirely.
TACENDUM_API=
TACENDUM_WS=

# The Apple Developer Team ID is deliberately NOT a variable here: Xcode
# cannot read a .env, so a value here would be one nothing consumes. For
# device builds it lives in app/ios/Tacendum.local.xcconfig (gitignored, same
# policy as .env), copied from app/ios/Tacendum.xcconfig.example — the
# committed schema, which records the setup step.
